WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.825
You updated WordPress.

2
00:00:02.075 --> 00:00:03.886
But what about the PHP running
it?

3
00:00:04.136 --> 00:00:06.998
WordPress, themes, and plugins
contain code.

4
00:00:07.248 --> 00:00:09.670
PHP runs their server-side code.

5
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.486
A green WordPress update badge

6
00:00:12.486 --> 00:00:14.752
does not confirm that PHP's

7
00:00:14.752 --> 00:00:16.565
security fixes are installed.

8
00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:19.234
The runtime can have a
vulnerability

9
00:00:19.234 --> 00:00:21.653
even when WordPress itself is
current.

10
00:00:21.903 --> 00:00:24.702
Start with Tools, Site Health,
Info, then Server.

11
00:00:24.952 --> 00:00:26.517
Note the PHP version.

12
00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:28.957
Then ask your host or

13
00:00:28.957 --> 00:00:30.709
server administrator to confirm

14
00:00:30.709 --> 00:00:32.461
the installed security fixes.

15
00:00:32.711 --> 00:00:35.389
Vendors may patch an older
package

16
00:00:35.389 --> 00:00:38.068
without adopting the newest PHP
version.

17
00:00:38.318 --> 00:00:40.970
Managed hosting may handle
updates for you.

18
00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.369
Before an upgrade,

19
00:00:42.369 --> 00:00:44.285
back up and test compatibility.

20
00:00:44.535 --> 00:00:46.398
Afterward, verify the PHP

21
00:00:46.398 --> 00:00:48.262
actually serving your site.
